body:
all of our dependencies are peer dependencies

one thing I need to point out is that this will have an impact on getting started experience, because `npm install --save angular2` will no longer be sufficient. I think that while getting started experience is important, setting up the dependencies correctly is even more important. We could file a bug against npm to have an option to install and save peer dependencies automatically.

// @alexeagle @wardbell @rkirov

> something like: npm install --save angular2 rxjs es6-shim zone.js

@IgorMinar I don't think it is that simple as we might have a situation where all the latest versions (ones resolved and installed by npm when no version is specified) might not work together. RxJS is the one that worries me the most here. I guess we are in the special period where several projects (zone, Rx, ng2) are moving very fast so probability of getting into troubles is higher. Unless we "lock" peer dependencies to a very narrow range.

I would vote for differing decision on  deps vs. peerDeps till we've got bundling story figured out since we need to look into the entire experience with ng2.
